A graph does not look like a matrix, yet every finite graph can be turned into one, and the eigenvalues of that matrix encode a surprising amount of the graph's combinatorial structure: how connected it is, whether it is bipartite, how many colors its vertices need, and even how densely it can possibly be built in the first place. This bridges graph theory with the theory of [eigenvalues and eigenvectors](https://raw.org/book/linear-algebra/eigenvalues-and-eigenvectors/).
